A||||21||MooCow||How to make my car handle better?||||3||
Z||000000||MooCow||09-02-2001||09:16 PM||||Well I don't feel like my Fiero is up to par.  My friends $500 Fiero can out handle my $4,700 Fiero and I hate that. Granted my car looks and is far faster  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  So I want to know what to look for to see if it needs replacement.  I was thinking of going all poly, but am unsure if I need to.  Also I dont hav tons of money to blow at the moment but would like to do it over time.  So what is the first thing I should look for on the suspension to make it handle better??<P>Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_7.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||3
Z||000001||BlackDragon||09-02-2001||09:22 PM||||shocks   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>im looking for a inexpensive black standard fastback. does not have to run. does not have to look good. only the mid section needs to be dent free. the fenders,bumpers, dont have to be in good cond. faded paint is fine. anyone who has info, email me. grey interior would be nice but beggars cant be choosers||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||09-02-2001||09:43 PM||||There are many things that will help your car handle better.  It helps to do everything all at once, but if that is not possible then I would go in this order:<P>-new shocks & struts<BR>-performance tires<BR>-ball joints<BR>-bushings<BR>-lowered springs<P>Go with a performance oriented shock/strut, not just a stock replacement....KYB's seem to have a good "bang for your buck". <P>Same goes for tires.......go with a H or higher speed rating with a good tread pattern.  <P>You'd be surprised what a good set of tires will do to your performance.<P>The rest should be done all at once.....ball joints, bushings & lowered springs.  It makes sense, since you will need to tear most of the front end apart to replace these parts (and you'll only need to get one alignment).<P>For the time being go for the shocks/struts and tires. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||09-02-2001||09:54 PM||||lower profile tires and definataly shocks.  Be sure to put larger tires (wider) on the back.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forsalestart.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Slammed Fiero||09-02-2001||10:01 PM||||Slamm the thing to the ground , Get some 40-45 series tires with a nice stiff sidewall. Make the rear of the car as stiff as nails , try to keep the front softer , this means no aftermarket lowering springs , there to stiff for the front of an 84 to 87 Fiero. Trust me I have had 3 sets!!! Koni's with ground control coilovers outback. Get a spring rate of 290 to 325lbs , 325 is pushing it for a street car. Set the koni's on full hard. , Toss the rubber bushings  , set the koni's upfront to 2 clicks from full soft.<P>Big bars front and rear don't use a massive bar outback and a small bar infront , this causes the car to have radical transitions. Get a good alignment , Moog problem solver ball joints , make sure the front bar is cranked fairly tight , the tighter the nut on the bar the sooner it will work. Losening or tightening the bolts a few threads can make a lot of difference<P>Do all of this and the Fiero will handle nothing like it does now. It will be Neutral , no understeer , no oversteer. You can play around with camber and the bar settings to adjust for your driving style. A little understeer is safest for a street car.<P><BR>I have been autocrossing my car for 5 years now , this is the best setup I have ever had. I have tried every lowering spring on the market , they are an improvement over stock stuff , but could be better. As for fancy tubular front ends and what not , you don't need them. Talk to guys who race their fiero , they will tell you stuff like that is just for show.<P>you can always join up to the Fiero racing list which I moderate , about 210 Fiero autocrossers , race enthuasists who talk about this stuff all day long , we have a lot of talent and it's where i learned to setup my car.<P>Search yahoogroups for "Fieroracinglist"<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MooCow||09-02-2001||11:06 PM||||Thanks for the info thus far.  I do know one thing for sure is that I am going to be getting 17 inch rims hoefully next year so I am not worried about my tires at the moment and yes my tires are pretty decent.  Also I am in no way going to lower my car.  MI roads suck for me to do this.  I will definetly hit things if I lower my car.<BR>So how hard is it to put on shocks?? Also struts??  Shocks Im guessing are just remove the old and put thesein. As far as struts go I dont think I know what one looks like.<BR>Keep em comming<BR>Slamed Fiero your setup is way out of my price range as of now.  Sounds very nice, but half of what you said sorta went over my head.  I hav some research to do now. hehe.<P>Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_7.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||1
Z||000006||MrPBody||09-02-2001||11:15 PM||||What do you mean when you say that your friend's Fiero out-handles yours?  What is your friend's Fiero?  What kind of tires and suspension does each car have?<P>Your car looks like it is already lowered, but maybe that's because it is parked in the grass.<P>If your shocks are sloppy, good shocks will make a big difference.  Good tires are like magic, too.  If your control arm bushings are worn, replace them at least with good stock bushings, or with polyurethane or IRM high-durometer bushings.<P>Handling modifications on my '85 SE:<P>    Mild lowering<P>    Good shocks<P>    Negative camber front and rear (adjustable upper ball joints on front)<P>    1" anti-roll bar on front; 7/8" anti-roll bar on rear (stock front bar installed on rear)<P><BR>And don't forget the cheapest handling improvement: check the air in your tires.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||09-03-2001||12:01 AM||||Hey, Jonathon, any suggestions for 88s?  How about alignment spec?  Just go with a factory alignment, or should the settings be tweaked?  Remember, at least for me, this is for the street, so an all-out autocross setup is not good.  Gotta get a little wear and tear outta my tires you know.||||reg||5
