Z||000011||Pontiaddict||09-03-2001||01:34 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Slamm the thing to the ground , Get some 40-45 series tires with a nice stiff sidewall. Make the rear of the car as stiff as nails , try to keep the front softer , this means no aftermarket lowering springs , there to stiff for the front of an 84 to 87 Fiero. Trust me I have had 3 sets!!! Koni's with ground control coilovers outback. Get a spring rate of 290 to 325lbs , 325 is pushing it for a street car. Set the koni's on full hard. , Toss the rubber bushings  , set the koni's upfront to 2 clicks from full soft.<P>Big bars front and rear don't use a massive bar outback and a small bar infront , this causes the car to have radical transitions. Get a good alignment , Moog problem solver ball joints , make sure the front bar is cranked fairly tight , the tighter the nut on the bar the sooner it will work. Losening or tightening the bolts a few threads can make a lot of difference<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So, what are you using for your soft sprung, yet lowered front end? You didn't mention that part.||||reg||1
