Z||000017||KRMFiero||09-03-2001||08:53 PM||||how do u put a front sway bar on the rear? I could take the sway bar of my 84 and stick it on my 86  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   what would i need to buy?<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000018||artherd||09-03-2001||09:25 PM||||Follow what Jonothan says for the 84-87s, good stuff.<P>For 88 cars, I have found that simply replacing springs/shocks (coilovers if you can) and bushings (poly, or preferably, deralin front, and spherical bearing rear... though this may be extreme for some.)<P>I have 15degrees toe-out in front, it does make the car slightly wandery, though never unstable even at 125+but also lets it turn very 'hard' and fast.    <BR> (DO NOT put larger than 7" wheels on the front of your 88, you WILL regret it   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  9" works fine on the rear however.  I speak only of 17+" wheels, that's another thing every 88 desperately needs   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I haven't gotten to the bars on my car yet, other than ajusting them.  As it's been said, just a few turns of those nuts makes a BIG difference.  I would never use the *same* bars front and rear on any mid-engined car, but agree that upgrading the bars both front *and* rear not only generally helps, but it is essential you upgrade both at the same time.<P>The 88 Fiero is already setup very well for awesome handling, a new set of springs/shocks (Koni and/or Held 12-way coilovers.) a set of poly/deralin, and some agressive 17x7 front, 17x8-9 rear and 215-45/4255-0 series rubber will do wonders.<P>Also, I recomend front-battery relocation (with RIGID mounting, of course) for *all* fieros, v8 or otherwise.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<P>[/b]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">   <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"<p>[This message has been edited by artherd (edited 09-03-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000019||Raydar||09-03-2001||10:15 PM||||Something that I've heard of on the pre-88 cars is to replace the rubber cradle bushings with solid aluminum ones. <BR>Several other Forum members have been very pleased with the results.<P>Any comments?<P>BTW... 88s have the cradle bolted straight to the body. No bushings at all.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000020||MrPBody||09-03-2001||10:43 PM||||Here's Randy Agee's description of mounting a stock front bar on the rear.   <A HREF="http://fierozone.tripod.com/weekend/swaybar/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fierozone.tripod.com/weekend/swaybar/index.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Will||09-05-2001||09:38 PM||||To make stock Fiero handle better:<P>1.  replace rubber cradle bushings with aluminum or poly<P>2.  replace rubber rear control arm bushings with poly/delrin/UHMW<P>The above two mods will dramatically increase the controllability of the car's back end, which I think is the biggest weakness of the stock setup. Once the back end is no longer likely to snap around, then add a rear anti-roll bar and go to hard bushings in the front suspension, upper control arms first.<P>When you get new wheels, size them so that the tire sizes are in proportion to the car's weight distribution: 45/55 weight dist -&gt; 215 front tires, 255 rear tires||||reg||1
